.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
*.log
runs/
data/our_pids.txt
data/*.log
data/s122_cache/
data/s122_progress.log

# Killing-tensor search caches: resumable computation artifacts, not results.
# kt_def_rows_r3.pkl alone is 172 MB (above GitHub's limit) and is regenerable in ~24 min.
# They must stay on disk for power-cut resumption, and must NOT be versioned.
data/*.pkl
# .npz added 2026-08-29 after `git add -A` committed 5 GB of solver checkpoints. The rule above
# covered .pkl and .ckpt -- the extensions the checkpoints used AT THE TIME. solve_kt_modp banks
# numpy arrays as .npz, a new extension the rule never anticipated, so the bulk arrays walked
# straight past it. Note this is the EXACT MIRROR of the 08-22 defect in this same file, where
# `data/kt_*.out` was too BROAD and swept up the run logs that were the only evidence for the
# results. Too broad hides the receipts; too narrow commits the bulk. Same file, opposite errors,
# one week apart, and neither was a judgement about evidentiary value -- both were about extensions.
data/*.npz
data/*.ckpt
# data/kt_*.out was ignored here until 2026-08-22. It should not have been. The rule above it
# targets the .pkl row-banks (2.6 GB, above GitHub's limit, regenerable); the .out RUN LOGS total
# 176 KB across 44 files and are the ONLY artifact carrying the numbers RESULTS 124 cites. A
# gitignore written for size swept up the receipts: every prover dimension in that section existed
# on one local disk and in no committed artifact. Found by running the bridge's provenance audit
# on ourselves after they ran it on themselves. The logs are now tracked.
data/s122_*.out
data/p0_*.out
